Well, last night I didn't feel like sleeping, so I decided to play our
favorite game.  Well, I finished it.  The whole schmear.  Victory at
last.  Amazing.  Finally.  I've been nursing this character along since
January.  This was 3.0i, playing a Wizard.  Strangely, it was the
third character I played since I installed.  Amusing to see scores of
1000, 2000, and 4,750,000 on the high score table....

Well-known spoilers



I didn't cheat on this one.  None of that copying of save files,
although I was in dread fear of power outages.  I knew how to get the
Wiz off my back permanently with a paralysis potion, but that was more
a bug than a feature, so I didn't do that.

I abused the heck out of this game.  I polypiled as much as possible,
mainly to get magic markers, a shield of reflection, better food (this
works really well), and a Hawaiian shirt.  I did the black pudding
trick to get resistances and (since there was an altar on the level)
all the artifacts.  After my character got up to a certain level,
there was no way to get up any further with experience points, so I
reverse-genocided wraiths (very uncommon, otherwise) and slept with
succubi (damn that sink was useful) to gain more experience levels.

I spent a lot of time making absolutely sure I would win.  Here's some
of my strategy:

I cleaned out absolutely the whole dungeon except the Wizard's level
before attempting to snag the amulet.  I used a pickaxe and assorted
wands of digging to clear lots of paths through the mazes of Hell.
This way, I had an easy time running out of the dungeon when escaping
with the Amulet.

I made sure my character was as powerful as possible.  I got a
blessed, +8 Frost Brand.  I got my AC down to -36.  I got my character
level to 30.  I got my HP's to 371 via nurses, potions of healing,
etc.  I got a shield of reflection.

The shield wasn't all that useful.  With my AC that good, blasts from
dragons, hell hounds, etc. never hit.  They just zipped past me.

I found only one shop (potions), but lots and lots of fountains, sinks,
altars, and thrones.  The thrones only rarely made themselves useful.
The altars were VERY useful, especially the lawful ones.  No priests
or oracles anywhere, either.  Foo.

Probably the most useful item after my speed boots was the luckstone I
found and blessed.  With that sucker, I always got good results with
praying, attacking, etc.  It gets up to +10 luck, I believe, which
means you have +10 to hit anything.  I never missed, after awhile.

Liches all got genocided.  Good riddance.  Gremlins got genocided, too.
Even better riddance.  Rust monsters didn't.  By the time that was an
option, I had mostly non-metallic armor, and rustproof weapons.  I
just took off the helmet and the shield and killed any rust monsters I
found.  Gelatinous cubes went later, after they ate one too many
potion and scroll I couldn't carry around.  After that, I couldn't
find much worth genociding.  Everything else was either food,
ungenocidable, or simply not dangerous enough to bother.  Blue dragons
were a pain, since they kept blinding me.  I should have nailed them,
too.  Being blind on the endgame is dangerous.

Once I had the stronghold cleared out, I used that level as home base.
I made stacks of everything:  tools here, amulets there, scrolls in
this corner, etc, etc.  It was nice to have everything in one place.
That's where I did my polypiling, too.

Monsters are much more dangerous when they are wielding weapons.  On
the way down, if I found some swords or such that I could not use, I
stuck them on the previous level or in a box so that monsters wouldn't
attack me with them.

I thought I would be clever and reduce the Wizard to a wimp with
Stormbringer instead of killing him outright, only to have him return.
This didn't work.  I used a stethescope on the Wiz, and discovered
that his HP's went down much more quickly than his levels, and he
didn't get them back.  I though the Wiz was supposed to teleport out
of the way then regenerate HP's when he was hurt badly enough.
Instead, he just kept after me until he died.  I mostly saw him only
on the endgame level (with no up staircase).  Maybe that had something
to do with it.  My best strategy seemed to be to just hack him to
pieces with Frost Brand.

I found a really good way to deal with black puddings:  Get a good,
rustproof weapon.  Get a wimpy dagger or knife.  Attack the puddings
occasionally with the dagger to get them to split in half, but without
killing them outright.  Use the good weapon to kill them when you have
enough.  If you have good armor and are invisible, attacks from the
puddings shouldn't be too dangerous.

A ring of levitation is practically essential for dealing with moats.
I ended up spending a wish on one.  This way, I could hover over a
moat and dip things into it.  Dipping things to get potions of water
and scrolls of blank paper is really useful.

Watch out for the trapdoors in the stronghold!  I got stuck on the
right side of the stronghold.  I had to dig my way to the bottom of
the maze, then use a wand of cold to get across the moat.  This way, I
could get around the castle, if not through it.

I collected four fake Amulets of Yendor, and named them so I knew
which ones were which.  The idea was that when the Wizard stole an
amulet, he would take the wrong one.  As it turned out, he never stole
anything.

Now that I have finished the game, I get to make some comments about
it:

The endgame WAS too easy with a ring of conflict.  In 3.1, this thing
really needs something reduced.  perhaps give it a limited range, or
else give monsters a saving throw based on their level.  That way, it
would be more useful on the lower levels when fighting hordes of orcs.

Character magic resistance shouldn't be an all-or-nothing affair, by
way of cloaks or gray dragon scale mail.  The way it is, the powerful
spell casters are a disaster if you meet them without magic
resistance, and are wimpy once you do have it.  All they can do is
summon other monsters and curse items you're carrying.  Magic
resistance should be an attribute.  It has a percentage chance of
working, depending on your experience level.  Wearing magic resistant
armor should add a fixed percentage to your resistant.  For instance,
a 20th level character could have 50% magic resistance, but putting on
a cloak of magic resistance boosts that to 75%.  Maybe the amount that
the armor boosts your armor class should depend on the enchantment
bonus on the armor.

This is in fact a problem with all the intrinsics (all or nothing).
Maybe you could gradually gain intrinsics by eating the right things
and going up levels.

Amulets (including lifesaving), gems, and gold are the only things
worth points when you escape.  Shouldn't the other goodies (weapons
(especially artifacts), potions, scrolls, spellbooks, wands, etc.,
etc. be worth points?  Maybe give them points based on what a
shopkeeper would give you for them.

Speaking of shopkeepers, I found a serious flaw with them.  After you
rob their shop, they usually go after you.  Well, this doesn't happen
if you are invisible.  Since they can detect invisible customers,
shouldn't they be able to chase after invisible thieves?

The Wizard let me run all the way from his level (42, of course) to
level 1 before showing up again.  He was all over me in the endgame,
though.  Why should he make himself so scarce in the normal dungeon?
Is this a bug, a feature, or just luck?

Once you get a good artifact (blessed +8 frost brand, in my case), it
makes hand-to-hand combat the best way to kill something.  Spells,
wands, etc. really don't do all that well by comparison for direct
combat.  Mayhaps the amount of damage from a spell or wand should
depend on the level of the character casting it.  Missile weapons,
especially, are useless, since they cause only minor damage.

Liches are a pain.  I'm happy I genocided them.

Gelatinous cubes are even more of a pain.  They eat everything, and
they are resistant to everything, and they paralyze you.  I found that
bopping them with Excalibur was effective so long as there was nothing
else dangerous on the level.  They are good at keeping you humble.

Giants, spinach, and royal jelly increase strength.  Succubi increase
wisdom.  Potions of enlightenment can help intelligence and wisdom.
What about dexterity and constitution? How can you (permanantly)
increase any of the other attributes short of a potion of gain ability
(preferably blessed)?  Speaking of that potion, the unblessed version
should ALWAYS increase some attribute.  If it tries to increase some
attribute that is maxed out, it should go on to the next one.  The
only time it shouldn't work is when ALL the attributes are maxed out.

The difficulty of the different levels could use a little adjustment.
Dungeon levels 1-4 are about the right difficulty for beginning
characters, but you can't stay too long without starving.  Levels 5-15
(or so) are much, much, too hard.  Once you get to 20 (or so), the
rest of the game is too easy.  There should always be a challenge.
The castle, though, is brilliant.  I loved that level.  Hell is not
hard, merely boring.  Vlad is a joke.  For being in such a prominent
place, he should be more than merely a powerful vampire.  He should
have other nasty things he can do to you than bite.  Also, most of the
demon lords were peaceful, even though I played a (neutral) wizard.

It should be possible to go up experience levels with experience
points.  10,000,000 experience points per each of the last 10 levels
is a bit extreme.

There needs to be an indication that a weapon is rustproof.  This may
only be possible after identifying it.

Being invisible seems to be the only way to survive the tough levels.
Otherwise, everyone gangs up on you.

Once you've picked up all the stuff lying on the ground, it's hard to
get more stuff (like scrolls!).  Maybe monsters should have a better
chance of carrying something.  Also, the level of the monster should
determine how good their stuff is.  This is the case with soldiers,
already.  Why not rate how valuable "stuff" is, and give more powerful
monsters a better chance of carrying the good stuff?

Floating eyes paralyze you for too long a time.

It's too easy to starve to death on the upper levels.  Make the
monsters more nutritious there.  Jackals are the only thing at all
useful.

When the Wizard duplicated himself (double trouble), his duplicate
didn't look like a Wizard.  It looked like an umber hulk.  Is this a
bug or a feature?  It should just be another Wizard.

The big limitation on spellcasting was forgetting.  Those power points
on the bottom of the screen were completely meaningless.  There needs
to be a way to address this.  Perhaps there should be a scroll of
restore memory (there IS one of amnesia).  I got to cast identify only
three times.

Gems are too hard to identify.  Dammit.  Maybe unicorns could help
with this.

There should be a way to get poison resistance short of eating
poisonous critters and suffering the effects.  Maybe some (appropriately
dangerous) creature's corpse should confer resistance without
poisoning you in the process.

Speaking of poisonous critters, pythons shouldn't be poisonous.
Neither should kobolds.

I had yellow dragon scale mail.  This should confer acid resistance.
As it is, there is no way to get acid resistance short of polymorphing
yourself.

Speaking of polymorphing, once you've polymorphed into some
spell-casting nasty, you should be able to cast spells yourself,
although you might not be able to control WHAT spell.  Maybe this
should be part of your normal attack.

Why do monsters wielding weapons do so MUCH damage?  Does the weapon
damage get added to the normal attack damage?  A dwarf with a mattock
is about the deadliest thing around to a mid-level character.  It can
do 30 or more points per whack.  Ouch!!

I found one major misfeature.  I managed to get teleport control by
eating a tengu.  Since I was playing a wizard, I would have gotten
this anyway at level 17 or so.  However, when I lost this level (due
to a wraith), I also lost teleport control.  This should not happen.

What the hell is a tengu, anyway?  My dictionary didn't have it.  The
game needs a "more information" option on that thing, and most other
obscure monsters.

If you feel obliged to followup to any of this, please delete the
stuff in which you are not interested, and change the subject line of
your post.

Due to the way you get back hit points normally, the ring of
regeneration was too useful early on, and too useless later.  Either
the ring or the way you get back HP's normally should be adjusted.
Without some sort of magic, it takes FOREVER for a low-level character
to regain his health.  You have to go to a new level with low hits
just to keep from starving (you can't wait until you're healthy).

Pickaxes are NEVER cursed.  Neither are lamps, that I've noticed.
They should be, occasionally, just to keep people on their toes.

You have to be hovering over a moat to dip things into it.  This is
ridiculous.  You should be able to use it when you are right next to
it, but not on that exact square.

There's no way to tell if some special water you found is holy or
unholy, short of identifying it.  Dropping it on an altar can change
it.  I suppose a pet could tell you....

When you are visible, monsters track (follow) you even when they are
far away.  Monsters should only chase after you when they see you,
unless you've got the aggravate attribute.  This would make the
aggravate attribute much more meaningful, and would make certain
levels easier.  After all, why should non-ESP monsters know where you
are?  You certainly don't know where they are!

You shouldn't have to pick up a whole pile of stuff if they are all
the same item.

